foreign  and I've been repairing and restoring  typewriters for nearly a decade and I'm  going to show you how to restore a 90  year old Royal typewriter this  particular machine was purchased new in  India by a family member of my client to  him it is a family or living these  serial number places it in the second  half of 1930 in its current condition it  is not a functioning machine  the first pieces to come off of the  typewriter the Platinum knobs as well as  the plant which is to be replaced  and then I'll remove the body shells  I'll also remove the paper table the  bail roller assembly paper tray and the  feed rollers  usually Carriage disassembly stops there  but on cases of really rough machines  such as this one the internal type bars  and the actuator rods are all removed  from the machine and set aside from  Individual cleaning  [Music]  the first step is to cast with an air  compressor to dislodge large sections of  dirt and debris  [Music]  here I'm using a wire brush attachment  higher drill to get into hard to reach  places  [Music]  after the bulk of the rust is removed  everything gets to pass with mineral  spirits with a soft Crystal thrust  after the cleaning is finished all of  the bare metal surfaces get wiped with  the red to remove any of the residue  thank you  the keyboard on this machine was also  intensely discolored  keyring is carefully removed from the  key top I'm using a special key ring  remover plier from the early 1900s  anytime that you're working with a glass  keyboard you run the risk of breaking  Keys due to the age of the tools and the  hardened rubber about half of this Glass  Circle in the keyboard ended up  shattering  once all of the keys are disable the  paper Legends are removed  foreign  circles also get cleaned here I'm using  a razor blade to scrape off all of the  accumulated corrosion that is stuck to  the surface of the glass then they are  each individually hand polished with a  fine grade Polishing Compound  if new glass Rings need to be fabricated  they are cut by hands from around a 16  inch thick piece of glass  this is a common glass standard  thickness or feature frames  foreign  any Springs that are missing on the  typewriter get replaced over time they  can sometimes stretch or can become  damaged if a machine Falls  Minnesota replaced  and I also create new feed welders using  old rubber hosing or sometimes layers of  heat shrink  now I install the paper table this is  the piece of metal that holds both the  feed rollers in place and guides the  paper around the platen  the most difficult part about assembling  Royal Typewriters are the ball bearings  here I'm using his drinking straw to  insert them into the proper placement  along the carriage rail  now the tricky part is the ribbon  vibrator or the ribbon lifting Fork here  you can see me attaching it with a small  counter pin  the key Legends also need to be  carefully placed so that they are  aligned vertically  these king ring needs to be seated  properly with the key rate installation  tool and then once each key top on the  machine is completely set the bottom  plate of the keyring installation tool  gets rotated to a flat portion of the  plate which is used to seat down all of  the tabs on the bottom of the key ring  firmly against the bottom of the key  cover  [Music]  the second and last step is to  reassemble everything completely  all typewriters are individually  adjusted so that the parts work well  together  so when you take certain parts from one  machine to fill into another machine  that is missing those parts you will  need to adjust those parts to work with  each other  once everything is carefully together I  install the brand new Rubber and the  feet as well as the new ribbon  [Music]  foreign  once the ribbon is in the machine the  type testing begins  the primary things that I'm looking for  are evenness of the letters alignment  between the capital and lowercase margin  consistency and line scale height  the escapement must also be adjusted so  that it is moving the proper distance  both up and down to prevent skipping or  missed spaces  then I move on to adjusting the height  of the ribbon to ensure that the entire  letter is being printed  the case is usually a secondary thing to  the typewriter that I generally don't  spend a lot of time with but this  particular one needed a little bit of  care unfortunately the based on the case  where it mounts to the machine had two  strips of water in place of the four  over feet that are supposed to be there  and there's no way to actually Mount the  machine to it so here I'm removing these  wood rails and then it's Drilling and  conscious sinking holes for the screws  and the feet that will Mount the machine  to the base  the final step is to cut a strip of  leather and to punch holes to stitch the  new handle onto the case  this was probably one of the worst rails  that I have seen cosmetically that being  said theoretically there is no amount of  damage that is physically irrepairable  however that does come a point when the  cost of repair far exceeds the value of  the machine both monetarily and  sentimentally  and in essence that is how I restore  antique typewriters any machine from the  mid-1800s to as late as 2020 follows  pretty much the same exact procedures  [Music]

